  "reasoning_content": "We need respond to user: \"Write a detailed tutorial on implementing a bounded LRU cache in Python using collections.OrderedDict. Include a complete class, explain get and put behavior, discuss edge cases, and include tests. Continue with a worked example and complexity analysis. Be precise and use meaningful prose and code.\"\n\nNeed produce final tutorial. Need think through content. Need ensure precise. Need include complete class, get/put, edge cases, tests, worked example, complexity.\n\nWe can implement LeetCode-like LRUCache: capacity int, get(key) returns value or -1, put(key,value) updates/inserts evicts oldest if over capacity. Use OrderedDict. In Python 3.7 dicts preserve insertion order and move_to_end added in 3.3? Actually collections.OrderedDict has move_to_end since 3.2? It's fine. We'll use OrderedDict.\n\nNeed include detailed explanation. Edge cases: capacity zero? In LRU cache capacity should positive but handle capacity <=0 maybe no storage. get missing returns default -1 (configurable maybe). put None? keys can be any hashable? OrderedDict keys hashable. value any object. Updating existing key should move to most recent. get moves to most recent. If capacity <=0, get returns default, put does nothing. If key exists and capacity zero? no-op. If capacity negative? treat as zero. Potential overflow? no. Thread-safety? OrderedDict not thread-safe; if concurrent use add lock. Could mention optional. Also if key unhashable raises TypeError from OrderedDict. If capacity too large MemoryError? Maybe mention.\n\nComplete class:\n\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    __slots__ = (\"capacity\", \"store\")\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int) -> None:\n        if capacity < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"capacity must be non-negative\")\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.store = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key, default=-1):\n        if key not in self.store:\n            return default\n        self.store.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.store[key]\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        if self.capacity == 0:\n            return\n        if key in self.store:\n            self.store[key] = value\n            self.store.move_to_end(key)\n            return\n        if len(self.store) >= self.capacity:\n            # pop",
